  <header class=3D"post-header">
    <h1 class=3D"post-title" itemprop=3D"name headline">So You Want To Retr=
ieve and Extract .tar.gz Archives with Empscripten</h1>
    <p class=3D"post-meta"><time datetime=3D"2020-01-26T00:00:00+00:00" ite=
mprop=3D"datePublished">Jan 26, 2020</time></p>
  </header>

 =20

  <div class=3D"post-content" itemprop=3D"articleBody">
    <p>As part of ongoing work with <a href=3D"https://mmore500.github.io/d=
ishtiny/">my scientific project=E2=80=99s cool cool web interface</a>, I sp=
ent this afternoon and evening figuring out how to download configuration a=
nd data files into the browser.
I figured I should share what I learned!</p>

<p>Emscripten=E2=80=99s nifty <a href=3D"https://emscripten.org/docs/portin=
g/files/packaging_files.html">file packaging</a>, which prepares elements o=
f the browser file system at compile time and packages it with your =E2=80=
=9Cexecutable,=E2=80=9D had been my go to for a while.
However, it became necessary to be able to swap out file contents without r=
ecompiling so this no longer cut the mustard.</p>

<p>The configuration and data files I work with are organized in a hierarch=
ical structure with meaningful filenames that have unpredictable components=
.
I could try to have the user specify the filenames one by one in the browse=
r then copy them down one by one, but that would be brittle and annoying.</=
p>

<p>Instead, I=E2=80=99d like to grab them all at once.
A <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tar_(computing)">tar archive</a>=
, which allows entire directory structures to be packaged into a single fil=
e, seems an appealing option here.</p>

<p>My files are big and repetitive, so I=E2=80=99d like to use <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gzip">gzip compression</a> so they=E2=80=99ll =
serve up over the internets faster.</p>

<p>Compressing and extracting <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter=
-rouge">.tar.gz</code> archives at the terminal is easy<a href=3D"https://x=
kcd.com/1168/">-ish</a>.
Getting it going from a C++ program inside Emscripten=E2=80=99s browser san=
dbox?
A little harder, but once I found the right pieces to slot together not act=
ually too bad!</p>

<p>In this blog, I=E2=80=99ll briefly discuss each the components I assembl=
ed and tweaked to create a minimal working example of retrieving and extrac=
ting a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.tar.gz</code> =
archive with Emscripten.
Then, I=E2=80=99ll walk you through getting the minimal working example act=
ually running on your own machine.</p>

<p>You can find all the complete minimal working example <a href=3D"https:/=
/github.com/mmore500/emscripten-targz">on Github</a>.</p>

<h2 id=3D"inflateh">
<a href=3D"https://mmore500.com/2020/01/26/emscripten-targz.html#inflateh" =
class=3D"toc-anchor">=F0=9F=94=97</a> <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">inflate.h</code>
</h2>

<p>The first step is to decompress (or =E2=80=9Cinflate=E2=80=9D) the archi=
ve.
The <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">zlib</code> projec=
t, which has <a href=3D"https://github.com/emscripten-ports/zlib">a maintai=
ned port for Emscripten</a>, provides a <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">gzFile_s</code> file handle analogous to the C-style <cod=
e class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">FILE</code> handle to get =
at a gzipped file.</p>

<p>So, we just do some <a href=3D"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10195=
343/copy-a-file-in-a-sane-safe-and-efficient-way">K &amp; R</a> B.S. to cop=
y content from the gzipped file over to a regular file.</p>

<h2 id=3D"untarh">
<a href=3D"https://mmore500.com/2020/01/26/emscripten-targz.html#untarh" cl=
ass=3D"toc-anchor">=F0=9F=94=97</a> <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">untar.h</code>
</h2>

<p>Now we just have a regular old <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlig=
hter-rouge">tar</code> file.
But we still have to do a little more work.
This header provides <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">u=
ntar</code>, which reads that <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter=
-rouge">tar</code> archive into the file system.</p>

<p>I borrowed most of the code in this header from Tim Kientzle=E2=80=99s <=
a href=3D"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/libarchive/libarchive/master/co=
ntrib/untar.c">untar</a> and added support for long filenames stored using =
<a href=3D"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2078778/what-exactly-is-the-=
gnu-tar-longlink-trick">@LongLink</a>.</p>

<p>Besides standard library components, <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">untar.h</code> has no dependencies.</p>

<h2 id=3D"maincc">
<a href=3D"https://mmore500.com/2020/01/26/emscripten-targz.html#maincc" cl=
ass=3D"toc-anchor">=F0=9F=94=97</a> <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">main.cc</code>
</h2>

<p>We use <a href=3D"https://emscripten.org/docs/api_reference/emscripten.h=
.html#c.emscripten_wget"><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-roug=
e">emscripten_wget</code></a> to copy the <code class=3D"language-plaintext=
 highlighter-rouge">.tar.gz</code> archive into Emscripten=E2=80=99s in-bro=
wser file system.
Then, we call <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">inflate<=
/code> and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">untar</code=
> in sequence.
Finally, we clean up the original <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlig=
hter-rouge">.tar.gz</code> file (don=E2=80=99t need it anymore!) and print =
some results to make sure everything worked.</p>

<p>This file contains the interesting code bits and bops you=E2=80=99d plug=
 into your project if you were trying to do this yourself, so I=E2=80=99ve =
included a listing here for your edification. <img class=3D"emoji" title=3D=
":apple:" alt=3D":apple:" src=3D"https://github.githubassets.com/images/ico=
ns/emoji/unicode/1f34e.png" height=3D"20" width=3D"20"></p>

